{
    "article_summary": "The article describes the first palladium-catalyzed thiocarbonylation of alkenes using CO2 as a carbonyl source to produce branched thioesters. By employing a specific N-P type carbazophosphine ligand (L1) and hydrosilane as a reductant, the authors achieved high regioselectivity (b/l > 99/1) and broad functional group tolerance, including basic substrates and internal alkenes. Mechanistic studies suggest the reaction proceeds via direct CO2 insertion into a Pd-C bond to form a Pd-carboxylate intermediate.",
